## Concepts You Need (3 min read)

**What is Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF)?**

CSRF attacks trick authenticated users into executing unwanted actions on web applications. When you're logged into bank.com, your browser automatically includes session cookies with every request to that domain. An attacker hosting malicious code at evil.com can create requests to bank.com that your browser sends with your legitimate credentials, executing operations you never intended.

**Why Does This Vulnerability Exist?**

Browsers enforce Same-Origin Policy for reading responses but not for sending requests. When your browser loads evil.com, JavaScript on that page can create POST requests to bank.com, and your browser automatically includes your bank.com session cookies. Without CSRF tokens, bank.com cannot distinguish legitimate requests (from your intentional clicks) from forged requests (from attacker's page).

**The CSRF Token Defense Mechanism**

CSRF tokens are unpredictable random values generated server-side, tied to your session, and required for all state-changing operations. The server embeds the token in rendered pages. When you submit a form, your browser includes both the session cookie (sent automatically) and the CSRF token (from the form field). The server validates both match. Attackers at evil.com cannot read your bank.com pages due to Same-Origin Policy, so they cannot obtain valid CSRF tokens, and their forged requests fail validation.

**Where Might I Encounter This?**

Any Spring Boot application with state-changing endpoints (POST, PUT, DELETE) is vulnerable without CSRF protection. Common targets include money transfers, password changes, account settings updates, email changes, and permission grants. WebSocket STOMP endpoints represent a particularly dangerous attack surface because they often bypass standard CSRF validation filters.

**Spring Framework STOMP Vulnerability (CVE-2023-34035)**

Spring Framework versions 5.3.0 through 5.3.30 and 6.0.0 through 6.0.13 contained a critical CSRF bypass in STOMP WebSocket message handling. The CsrfChannelInterceptor failed to validate tokens for STOMP CONNECT frames, allowing attackers to establish WebSocket connections and send messages without valid CSRF tokens. This affected real-time features like chat, notifications, and collaborative editing.

