## Common Mistakes & Solutions

### Mistake 1: Using GET for State-Changing Operations

```java
// ❌ WRONG: GET request changes state (violates HTTP semantics)
@GetMapping("/api/transfers/send")
public ResponseEntity<String> transfer(
        @RequestParam String toAccount,
        @RequestParam BigDecimal amount) {
    accountService.transferFunds(getCurrentUser(), toAccount, amount);
    return ResponseEntity.ok("Transfer completed");
}

// ✅ CORRECT: Use POST for state-changing operations
@PostMapping("/api/transfers/send")
public ResponseEntity<String> transfer(@RequestBody TransferRequest request) {
    // CSRF token automatically validated by Spring Security
    accountService.transferFunds(getCurrentUser(), request.getToAccount(), request.getAmount());
    return ResponseEntity.ok("Transfer completed");
}
```

### Mistake 2: Assuming SameSite=Lax Is Sufficient

```properties
# ❌ INSUFFICIENT: Lax allows cookies on top-level GET navigation
server.servlet.session.cookie.same-site=lax

# ✅ SECURE: Strict blocks all cross-site cookie sending
server.servlet.session.cookie.same-site=strict
```

**Why Lax is insufficient:**
- Lax allows cookies on top-level navigation (clicking links)
- Attacker can use `<a href="https://bank.com/api/transfers/send?to=attacker&amt=500">Click here</a>`
- If endpoint accepts GET (mistake #1), attack succeeds
- Strict blocks ALL cross-site requests, preventing this

### Mistake 3: Not Protecting WebSocket Endpoints

```java
// ❌ WRONG: STOMP endpoint without CSRF validation
@Configuration
@EnableWebSocketMessageBroker
public class WebSocketConfig implements WebSocketMessageBrokerConfigurer {
    // No configureClientInboundChannel override = no CSRF validation
}

// ✅ CORRECT: Add CsrfChannelInterceptor
@Configuration
@EnableWebSocketMessageBroker
public class WebSocketConfig implements WebSocketMessageBrokerConfigurer {

    @Override
    public void configureClientInboundChannel(ChannelRegistration registration) {
        registration.interceptors(
            new SecurityContextChannelInterceptor(),
            new CsrfChannelInterceptor()  // ✅ Validates CSRF tokens
        );
    }
}
```

### Mistake 4: Storing CSRF Token Only in Cookie

```java
// ❌ VULNERABLE: Token only in cookie (double-submit pattern)
// If XSS exists, attacker reads cookie and includes in request

// ✅ SECURE: Token in cookie (readable by JS) + server-side session validation
// Even if XSS reads cookie, token must match server-side session value
@Bean
public CsrfTokenRepository csrfTokenRepository() {
    CookieCsrfTokenRepository repository = CookieCsrfTokenRepository.withHttpOnlyFalse();
    repository.setCookieHttpOnly(false);  // Allow JS to read for header inclusion
    return repository;
}
// Spring Security validates cookie token against session-stored token
```

### Mistake 5: Excluding DELETE/PUT from CSRF Validation

```java
// ❌ WRONG: Only protecting POST requests
http.csrf(csrf -> csrf
    .requireCsrfProtectionMatcher(request -> request.getMethod().equals("POST"))
);

// ✅ CORRECT: Protect all state-changing methods
http.csrf(csrf -> csrf
    .requireCsrfProtectionMatcher(request -> {
        String method = request.getMethod();
        return method.equals("POST") || method.equals("PUT") || 
               method.equals("DELETE") || method.equals("PATCH");
    })
);
```

